{"name":"Munchkin","hex":"#9bb139","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Munchkin","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A domestic cat breed with short legs."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Munchkin"}],"entries":[{"title":"Munchkin","blocks":[{"text":"A Munchkin is a native of the fictional Munchkin Country in the Oz books by American author L. Frank Baum. Although a common fixture in Germanic fairy tales, they are introduced to modern audiences with the first appearance in the classic children's novel The Wonderful Wizard of Oz (1900) where they welcome Dorothy Gale to their city in Oz. The Munchkins are described as being the same height as Dorothy and they wear only shades of blue clothing, as blue is the Munchkins' favorite color. Blue is also the predominating color that officially represents the eastern quadrant in the Land of Oz. The Munchkins have appeared in various media, including the 1939 film The Wizard of Oz, as well as in various other films and comedy acts."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Munchkins.png/330px-Munchkins.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Munchkin","wikidata":"Q8018058"},{"title":"Munchkin (card game)","blocks":[{"text":"Munchkin is a dedicated deck card game by Steve Jackson Games, written by Steve Jackson and illustrated by John Kovalic. It is a humorous take on role-playing games, based on the concept of munchkins."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/e/ee/Munchkin_game_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Munchkin_(card_game)","wikidata":"Q729935"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Richard_Wagner_M%C3%BCnchner_Kindl_am_Fa%C3%9F.jpg/500px-Richard_Wagner_M%C3%BCnchner_Kindl_am_Fa%C3%9F.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Richard_Wagner_M%C3%BCnchner_Kindl_am_Fa%C3%9F.jpg/500px-Richard_Wagner_M%C3%BCnchner_Kindl_am_Fa%C3%9F.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Depiction of the Münchner Kindl in an advertisement c. 1910.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARichard_Wagner_M%C3%BCnchner_Kindl_am_Fa%C3%9F.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/3/32/Munchkins-film.jpg/330px-Munchkins-film.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/3/32/Munchkins-film.jpg/330px-Munchkins-film.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Munchkins (specifically the \"Lollipop Guild\") as depicted in the 1939 film The Wizard of Oz. L–R: Jackie Gerlich, Jerry Maren, and Harry Doll.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMunchkins-film.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/The_Munchkins_Star.jpg/500px-The_Munchkins_Star.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/The_Munchkins_Star.jpg/1280px-The_Munchkins_Star.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/The_Munchkins_Star.jpg/500px-The_Munchkins_Star.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Star on the Hollywood Walk of Fame","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Munchkins_Star.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Munchkin_card_game_being_played_%2C_March_2014.jpeg/500px-Munchkin_card_game_being_played_%2C_March_2014.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb